## Git commands

The repo is initialized on `main` with one commit; no remote is configured. `git push` to a protected branch is blocked by this role's guardrail, and you didn't supply a URL, so these are for you to run — substitute `<OWNER>`:

```bash
cd my_website
git remote add origin https://github.com/<OWNER>/my_website.git
git push -u origin main
```

If the existing repo already has commits (e.g. just `info.md`), reconcile first rather than clobbering it:

```bash
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main && git push -u origin main
```

Run all existing workflows — `workflow_dispatch` is now on the workflow, so either:

```bash
gh workflow run deploy.yml --repo <OWNER>/my_website --ref main
gh run list --repo <OWNER>/my_website --workflow deploy.yml   # watch it
```

or without `gh`:

```bash
curl -L -X POST \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $GH_PAT" \
  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
  -H "X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2022-11-28" \
  https://api.github.com/repos/<OWNER>/my_website/actions/workflows/deploy.yml/dispatches \
  -d '{"ref":"main"}'
```

The PAT needs `actions:write` only — never `repo` write, and never paste it into the workflow file.

Two things left for you, both outside what I can do here: set **Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source: GitHub Actions**, and set `SITE.url` in `build.js` to your real Pages URL so canonical links are correct. If you pushed before this fix, treat anything that was in the repo as disclosed — `SECURITY.md` has the rotation and history-purge procedure.

